 ## Background
-Line Bundles (also known as Fiber Bundles) was developed by R. Crawfis, H. Shen, and N. Max [S1, unofficial]. The technique was presented in the context of flow visualization techniques for CFD using volume rendering [S1, unofficial] and was applied to visualizing 3D velocity fields near contour surfaces [S2]. The authors developed several more techniques to represent flow fields using anisotropic or textured volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. The technique relies on graphics hardware to create texture by rendering and compositing many semitransparent line segments [S1, unofficial]. It extends the concept of splats to composite collections of antialiased lines in a back-to-front manner [S2].
+Line Bundles (also known as Fiber Bundles) was developed by R. Crawfis, H. Shen, and N. Max [S1, unofficial]. The technique was presented in the context of flow visualization techniques for CFD using volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. It was applied to visualizing 3D velocity fields near contour surfaces [S2]. The authors developed several more techniques to represent flow fields using anisotropic or textured volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. The technique relies on graphics hardware to create texture by rendering and compositing many semitransparent line segments [S1, unofficial]. It extends the concept of splats to composite collections of antialiased lines in a back-to-front manner [S2].
 
